Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
  "Week 2   Projective Geometry\n",
  StyleBox["Extra Material on Complex Numbers",
    FontSize->18]
}], "Title",
  Evaluatable->False,
  PageBreakAbove->True,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\[Copyright] Dana Scott 1993/1997/1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\n\nNotebook  last edited: ",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["6 September 1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Subsubtitle"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Roots of Unity, Again", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Relating them", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Question 1.", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Look again at the ",
  StyleBox["5th",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " roots of ",
  StyleBox["unity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and of ",
  StyleBox["minus unity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(RootPlot[z\^5 == 1, z, 10, \n\t
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-1.1\), 1.1}, {\(-1.1\), 1.1}}]; \n
    RootPlot[z\^5 == \(-1\), z, 10, \n\t
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-1.1\), 1.1}, {\(-1.1\), 1.1}}]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Animate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the two pictures and ask how the two arrangements of points are ",
  StyleBox["related",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". And generalize this question to any ",
  StyleBox["odd",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " power."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Answer", "Subsubsection",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n == 2\ k + 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is an odd number, then ",
  StyleBox["z", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is an ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " root of ",
  StyleBox["+1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " if, and only if, ",
  StyleBox["-z", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is an ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "root of ",
  StyleBox["-1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  This is obvious because, by algebra, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\((\(-z\))\)\^n == \(-z\^n\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", when ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is odd. (This was also obvious in the pictures.)"
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Question 2.", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Look again at the ",
  StyleBox["6th",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " roots of ",
  StyleBox["unity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and of ",
  StyleBox["minus unity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(RootPlot[z\^6 == 1, z, 10, \n\t
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-1.1\), 1.1}, {\(-1.1\), 1.1}}]; \n
    RootPlot[z\^6 == \(-1\), z, 10, \n\t
      PlotRange \[Rule] {{\(-1.1\), 1.1}, {\(-1.1\), 1.1}}]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Animate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the two pictures and ask how the two arrangements of points are ",
  StyleBox["related",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". And generalize this question to any ",
  StyleBox["even",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " power."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Answer", "Subsubsection",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Ah, ha, the relationship is not so easy, since if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n == 2\ k\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is an even number, then  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\((\(-z\))\)\^n == z\^n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and the minus trick does ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " work.  But the ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " roots of unity \[LongDash] or any other complex number of absolute value \
",
  StyleBox["1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " \[LongDash] always form a ",
  StyleBox["regular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["n-gon",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Therefore, any one picture is the ",
  StyleBox["rotation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of another one.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "From the animation, we see that the angle of rotation is ",
  StyleBox["half",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the angle used in forming the ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-gon.  So, let ",
  StyleBox["w", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " be a ",
  StyleBox["kth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " root of ",
  StyleBox["I", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Then, ",
  StyleBox["z", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is an ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " root of ",
  StyleBox["+1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " if, and only if, ",
  StyleBox["w*z", "InlineInput"],
  " is an ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "root of ",
  StyleBox["-1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  This is (almost) obvious because, by algebra, we have:\n            ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(\((w\ z)\)\^n\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(\(w\^n\) z\^n\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], \(\(w\^\(2\ k\)\) z\^n\), "==", 
        \(\(w\^\(k\ 2\)\) z\^n\), "==", \(\(I\^2\) z\^n\), "==", 
        \(-z\^n\)}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ",",
  StyleBox["\n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "because ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is even.  So, one picture is the rotation of the other by the angle of ",
  StyleBox["w", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the way, it is easy to check that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(w\^2\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is an ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " root of unity.  If we take ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " with the ",
  StyleBox["least positive angles",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(w\^2 == z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["w", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " has ",
  StyleBox["half",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the angle of ",
  StyleBox["z", "InlineInput"],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Adding them", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Question 1.", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "What is the ",
  StyleBox["sum",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of all the ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " roots of ",
  StyleBox["unity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Answer", "Subsubsection",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],

Cell["Here are two trials.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plus@@\((z /. Solve[z\^5 == 1, z])\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plus@@\((z /. Solve[z\^6 == 1, z])\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Hmmm.  The first is not too helpful in that form.  Do it \
again.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plus@@\((z /. Solve[z\^5 == 1, z])\) // ComplexSimplify\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That is better.  The ",
  StyleBox["conjecture",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is that the sum is ",
  StyleBox["always",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Because of the regular arrangement, we can always let ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " be the root with ",
  StyleBox["least positive angle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and then the whole set of roots is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({1, z, z\^2,  ... , z\^\(n - 1\)}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Let is look at a particular sum in algebraic form."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sum7 = Plus@@\(z\^Range[0, 6]\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We are thinking here of the ",
  StyleBox["7th",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " roots of unity.  Let us multiply by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1 - z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (This is an old algebraic trick!)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((1 - z)\) sum7 // Expand\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Yes!  That ",
  StyleBox["must be ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Question 2.", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "What is the ",
  StyleBox["sum",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of all the ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " roots of ",
  StyleBox["minus unity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Answer", "Subsubsection",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " algebra will not work.  But if the set ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({1, z, z\^2,  ... , z\^\(n - 1\)}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " gives the ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " roots of unity, then, by a suitable choice of ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", the set \n                    ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["{",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["w",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[",",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[\(w\ z\),
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[",",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[
            RowBox[{"w", 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[\(z\^2\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}],
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[",",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["...",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[",",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[\(w\ z\^\(n - 1\)\),
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
        StyleBox["}",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  "\ngives the ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " roots of ",
  StyleBox["minus unity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", as we saw earlier.  The sum is again ",
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". (Why?)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the way, if the complex numbers (",
  StyleBox["points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ") ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({z\_0, z\_1, z\_2,  ... , z\_\(n - 1\)}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " form a regular ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-gon, then the average  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(\(z\_0 + z\_1 + z\_2 +  ... \) + z\_\(n - 1\)\)\/n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \[LongDash] taken as a vector sum \[LongDash]is the point at the ",
  StyleBox["center",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the polygon (",
  StyleBox["the center of gravity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ").  With the roots of ",
  StyleBox["\[PlusMinus]1", "InlineInput"],
  ", the center is at ",
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  This is another (geometric) reason why the sum of the roots of unity or \
minus unity must be ",
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ". "
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Multiplying them", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Question 1.", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "What is the ",
  StyleBox["product",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of all the ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " roots of ",
  StyleBox["unity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Answer", "Subsubsection",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],

Cell["We do some trials.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Times@@\((z /. Solve[z\^6 == 1, z])\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Times@@\((z /. Solve[z\^7 == 1, z])\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Table[Times@@\((z /. Solve[z\^n == 1, z])\), {n, 1, 10}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["conjecture",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is that the product is ",
  StyleBox["1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["odd",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["-1", "InlineInput"],
  " if ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["even",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is the algebraic proof.  If ",
  StyleBox["z", "InlineInput"],
  " is an ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " root of unity, then so is ",
  StyleBox["Conjugate[z]", "InlineInput"],
  StyleBox[
  ", in view of the automorphism principle.  But, we know,\n                 \
",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z\ Conjugate[z] == Abs[z]\^2 == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[",\nin this case.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, when ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["odd",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the roots of unity group themselves into ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(n - 1\)\/2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " conjugate ",
  StyleBox["pairs",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of complex numbers together with the real number ",
  StyleBox["+1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Clearly, then, the product of all of them is ",
  StyleBox["+1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next, when ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["even",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the roots of unity group themselves into ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(n - 2\)\/2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " conjugate ",
  StyleBox["pairs",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of complex numbers together with the ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " real number ",
  StyleBox["\[PlusMinus]1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Clearly, then, the product of all of them is ",
  StyleBox["-1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Question 2.", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "What is the ",
  StyleBox["product",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of all the ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " roots of ",
  StyleBox["minus unity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Answer", "Subsubsection",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],

Cell["We do some trials, as before.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Table[Times@@\((z /. Solve[z\^n == \(-1\), z])\), {n, 1, 10}]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["conjecture",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is that the product is ",
  StyleBox["-1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["odd",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["1", "InlineInput"],
  " if ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["even",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Algebra to the rescue.  If ",
  StyleBox["z", "InlineInput"],
  " is an ",
  StyleBox["nth",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " root of minus unity, then so is ",
  StyleBox["Conjugate[z]", "InlineInput"],
  StyleBox[
  ", again, in view of the automorphism principle.  And, we know that\n       \
          ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z\ Conjugate[z] == Abs[z]\^2 == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[",\nas before.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, when ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["odd",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the roots of minus unity group themselves into ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(n - 1\)\/2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " conjugate ",
  StyleBox["pairs",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of complex numbers together with the real number ",
  StyleBox["-1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Clearly, then, the product of all of them is ",
  StyleBox["-1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next, when ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["even",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", none of the the roots of minus unity is ",
  StyleBox["real",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", so they group themselves into ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n\/2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " conjugate ",
  StyleBox["pairs",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of complex numbers. Clearly, then, the product of all of them is ",
  StyleBox["+1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Mandelbrot Set", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Definitions", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Mandelbrot", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "See the material from Bernt Wahl: ",
  StyleBox["Exploring Fractals on the Macintosh",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " given out in class for historical background."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "At the height of the excitement over \"chaos theory\", the ",
  StyleBox["Economist",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " magazine said that the Mandelbrot set is \"the most complicated thing \
ever studied by mathematicians.\"  This is nonsense, of course.  Despite the \
fact that many properties of the Mandelbrot set are quite hard to prove (say, \
that it is topologically simply connected), it has an easy definition.  \
Despite the fact that it is very wiggly on its boundary, there are \
higher-dimensional analogues that are even more complicated and harder to \
analyze.  And this complication is nothing compared to many questions faced \
by mathematicians in topological dynamics, for example.  But, let us not \
disparage the good set, and let us see how it is defined using ",
  StyleBox["complex numbers",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A point ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (a complex number) is in the Mandelbrot set ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " if, and only if, the iteration  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z\_0 = \ 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z\_\(n + 1\) = z\_n\%2 + c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  remains ",
  StyleBox["bounded",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in absolute value for all ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "On a computer it is hard to carry out an ",
  StyleBox["infinite",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " iteration, so we are glad that there is a result (which we cannot prove \
here) which helps limit drastically ",
  StyleBox["some",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the computation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The above iteration \[LongDash] for a given number ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " \[LongDash] is ",
  StyleBox["unbounded",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if, and only if at some stage ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput"],
  " we find ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\( | z\_n | \( > \ 2\)\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that the automorphism principle for the ",
  StyleBox["Conjugate", "InlineInput"],
  " operation implies:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proposition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A given number ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is in the Mandelbrot set ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  if, and only if  ",
  StyleBox["Conjugate[c]", "InlineInput"],
  " is also in the set."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The reason is that the conjugate of the iteration for ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is is the iteration for ",
  StyleBox["Conjugate[c]", "InlineInput"],
  ".  So, one is bounded if, and only if, the other is.  In other words, the \
Mandelbrot set is ",
  StyleBox["symmetric",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " about the real axis."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Julia", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There are closely related sets called ",
  StyleBox["Julia sets",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Given a complex number ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " , the Julia set ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(J\_c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " consists of those complex numbers ",
  StyleBox["d", "InlineInput"],
  " such that the iteration  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z\_0 = \ d\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{" ", 
        StyleBox[\(z\_\(n + 1\) = z\_n\%2 + c\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  "  remains ",
  StyleBox["bounded",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in absolute value for all ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["The following statements are easy to prove.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proposition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A given number ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is in the Mandelbrot set ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  if, and only if  ",
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput"],
  " is in the Julia set ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(J\_c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proposition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A complex number ",
  StyleBox["d", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is in the Julia set ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(J\_c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  if, and only if  ",
  StyleBox["-d", "InlineInput"],
  " is in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(J\_c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In other words, every Julia set is ",
  StyleBox["symmetric",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " about origin ",
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[".", "InlineInput"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Some computations", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Mandelbrot", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There are ",
  StyleBox["scores",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of programs for various computers that do the computations and the \
graphics for the Mandelbrot and Julia sets very, very efficiently.  However, \
we can do some limited, but interesting exploration in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with actually very little programming \[LongDash] in order to illustrate \
the use of ",
  StyleBox["complex arithmetic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Since, we will have to do a lot of iteration, we remove ",
  StyleBox["for the time being",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " any built-in limits on recursion."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\($RecursionLimit = \[Infinity]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " be a given, fixed ",
  StyleBox["complex number",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Let ",
  StyleBox["i", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " be a ",
  StyleBox["cut-off value",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for doing an iteration.  Then, a ",
  StyleBox["stage",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "of the iteration is said to be ",
  StyleBox["good",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "if either the cut-off has been reached or if the ",
  StyleBox["value",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["z", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "of the iteration is small in absolute value ",
  StyleBox["and",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the ",
  StyleBox["next",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " iteration is still good.  Here is a ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " definition.  ",
  "The way the command ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Good[0, c, 0, 100]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " works is that it starts the iteration from ",
  StyleBox["0", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and as soon as a value is found of absolute value greater than ",
  StyleBox["2", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", is says ",
  StyleBox["False", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  However, if the absolute-value limit is not exceeded by stage ",
  StyleBox["100", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", it says ",
  StyleBox["True", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Good[n_, c_, z_, i_] := 
      If[\tn == i, \ True, \n\t
        If[Abs[z] > 2, False, Good[n + 1, c, z\^2 + c, i]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can make a table of calculations for some of the reals ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " between ",
  StyleBox["-3", "InlineInput"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["+3", "InlineInput"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Table[{c, Good[0, c, 0, 100]}, {c, \(-3\), 3, 0.1}] // TableForm\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And  here is a table of calculations for some complex ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " between ",
  StyleBox["-3-3 I", "InlineInput"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["3+3 I", "InlineInput"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Table[{c + I\ c, Good[0, c\  + \ I\ c, 0, 100]}, \n
        \t\t{c, \(-3\), 3, 0.1}] // TableForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Looking at the two tables we calculated, we can say that all ",
  StyleBox["False", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " entries are ",
  StyleBox["definitely",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["not in",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the Mandelbrot set.  The ",
  StyleBox["True", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " entries, on the other hand, are ",
  StyleBox["likely",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to be in the set, ",
  StyleBox["but we are not 100% sure",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  To be more careful, more calculation is needed.  For example, in the \
interval from ",
  StyleBox["-2.5", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to ",
  StyleBox["+1", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we can up the cut-off limit, take smaller steps, and we find:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Table[{c, Good[0, c, 0, 200]}, {c, \(-2.5\), 1, 0.05}] // TableForm\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, we see that many, many numbers outside the interval from ",
  StyleBox["-2", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to ",
  StyleBox["+0.25", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are in fact excluded.  And indeed, if a ",
  StyleBox["positive",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " number is excluded, then so are all ",
  StyleBox["larger",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " numbers.  (Why?) It is also easy to see that any number less that ",
  StyleBox["-2", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is also excluded. Here are some calculations."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(NestList[#\^2 - 2&, 0, 10] // TableForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The iteration for ",
  StyleBox["c==-2", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["stablizes",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at ",
  StyleBox["2", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  So ",
  StyleBox["-2", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is definitely ",
  StyleBox["in",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the Mandelbrot set.  Here is a number just below."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(NestList[#\^2 - 2.0001&, 0, 10] // TableForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "At stage ",
  StyleBox["n==2", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", the value is already over ",
  StyleBox["2", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  It then inches up, and as soon it gets to ",
  StyleBox["3", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " it ",
  StyleBox["explodes",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  So, ",
  StyleBox["-2.0001", "InlineInput"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["not in",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the set.  But will it ",
  StyleBox["always",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " explode eventually for any number below ",
  StyleBox["-2", "InlineInput"],
  "?  Here is a ",
  StyleBox["symbolic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " computation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(NestList[#\^2 - 2 - 1\/k&, 0, 10] // ExpandAll\) // TableForm\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Hmm.  It is a little hard to read.  It looks as though when ",
  StyleBox["k", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["positive",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the iteration for the choice of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c == \(-2\) - 1\/k\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " exceeds ",
  StyleBox["2", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " at stage ",
  StyleBox["n==2", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and then it inches up at stage ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " by more than some quantity ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(m\_n\/k\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(m\_n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a strictly increasing integer sequence.  If we could ",
  StyleBox["prove",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that, we would know that ",
  StyleBox["every",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " number less than ",
  StyleBox["-2", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is excluded.  (Why?) And, probably the proof is not too difficult!  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us examine next ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c == 1\/4\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(NestList[#\^2 + 1\/4&, 0, 10] // TableForm\n\), 
    \(\(% // N\) // TableForm\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It looks as though the iterates are inching up very slowly \[LongDash] but \
will they become unbounded?  We look at some ",
  StyleBox["critical ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "points for the iteration."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[z == z\^2 + 1\/4, z]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Of course!  We should have seen before that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is an important point.  Obviously, if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(0 \[LessEqual] z \[LessEqual] 1\/2\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z\^2 + 1\/4 \[LessEqual] 1\/2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Therefore, the iterates for ",
  StyleBox["all numbers",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " where we have ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(0 \[LessEqual] c \[LessEqual] 1\/4\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " must be ",
  StyleBox["bounded",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  For larger ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput"],
  ", we can check the critical points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[Apart, Solve[z == z^2 + 1/4 + 1/k, z], {3}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "These are complex, and so we do not get any hint about bounds for \
convergence.  Let's do an example.  It takes many iterations to get above ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  It takes even more to ",
  StyleBox["explode",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Nest[N[#\^2 + 0.25000001, 30]&, 0, 20000]\n\), 
    \(Nest[N[#\^2 + 0.25000001, 30]&, 0, 30000]\n\), 
    \(Nest[N[#\^2 + 0.25000001, 30]&, 0, 31000]\n\), 
    \(Nest[N[#\^2 + 0.25000001, 30]&, 0, 32000]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
A symbolic computation gives more of an indication as to what \
happens.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(NestList[#1\^2 + 0.25 + 1/k&, 0, 10] // ExpandAll\) // TableForm\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["\<\
One has to be careful about interpreting floating-point, \
approximate computations, but the above seems to give a good hint that there \
is inching up in a way that will finally explode.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next, we try a ",
  StyleBox["complex",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(c = 0.5\ I + 0.2; \n
    If[Good[0, c, 0, 200], \n\tnl = NestList[#1\^2 + c&, 0, 300]; \n\t
      \(ArgandPlot[nl, 4, AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
          PlotRange -> {{\(-2\), 2}, {\(-2\), 2}}];\), \n\t\t"\<BAD\>"]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["\<\
It would seem that the iterates converge to a limit (and, hence, \
are bounded).  A small change is bad, however.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(c = 0.6\ I + 0.2; \n
    If[Good[0, c, 0, 200], \n\tnl = NestList[#1\^2 + c&, 0, 300]; \n\t
      \(ArgandPlot[nl, 4, AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
          PlotRange -> {{\(-2\), 2}, {\(-2\), 2}}];\), \n\t\t"\<BAD\>"]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can see the iterates do escape from the circle ",
  StyleBox["|z|==2", "InlineInput"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(c = 0.6\ I + 0.2; \nnl = NestList[#1\^2 + c&, 0, 16]; \n\t
    ArgandPlot[nl, 4, AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}]; \)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Julia", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The computations for particular starting points for Julia sets are very \
similar to what we just tried out for the Mandelbrot set.  So, there is not \
much point showing any here.  It will be more interesting to look at the \
comparative behavior of inputs over a whole ",
  StyleBox["square of values",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in the complex plane."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Some broader pictures", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Mandelbrot", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
First, a rather rough picture of the set, obtained from just \
checking whether points are good or bad.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(DensityPlot[
      If[Good[0, x + I\ y, 0, 100], 0, 1], {x, \(-\(9\/4\)\), 3\/4}, {y, 
        \(-\(3\/2\)\), 3\/2}, \n\tMesh \[Rule] False, PlotPoints \[Rule] 50]; 
    \)\)], "Input",
  AnimationCycleOffset->1,
  AnimationCycleRepetitions->Infinity],

Cell["To get more detail, more plot points are required.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(DensityPlot[
      If[Good[0, x + I\ y, 0, 100], 0, 1], {x, \(-\(9\/4\)\), 3\/4}, {y, 
        \(-\(3\/2\)\), 3\/2}, \n\tMesh \[Rule] False, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 200]; \)\)], "Input",
  AnimationCycleOffset->0,
  AnimationCycleRepetitions->0],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can see even more detail by zooming in on a ",
  StyleBox["smaller square",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note:",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The following picture takes quite a long time to compute."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(DensityPlot[
      If[Good[0, x + I\ y, 0, 100], 0, 1], {x, \(-\(1\/2\)\), 1\/2}, {y, 
        1\/4, 5\/4}, \n\tMesh \[Rule] False, PlotPoints \[Rule] 200]; \)\)], 
  "Input",
  AnimationCycleOffset->0,
  AnimationCycleRepetitions->0],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Well, we have shown that ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can ",
  StyleBox["prototype",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " such computations, but special-purpose software is to be preferred for \
efficient displays."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Julia", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To get a picture of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(J\_c\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", keep ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["fixed",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and vary the ",
  StyleBox["starting point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the iteration.  Here is an example."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(c = 0.5\ I\  - 0.1; \n
    DensityPlot[
      If[Good[0, c, x + I\ y, 100], 0, 1], {x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, \n
      \tMesh \[Rule] False, PlotPoints \[Rule] 50]; \)], "Input",
  AnimationCycleOffset->1,
  AnimationCycleRepetitions->Infinity],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That picture is quite crude, since we used only a 50x50-mesh.  Here is a \
more refined picture at a more interesting value of ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(c = 0.75\ I\  - 0.1; \n
    DensityPlot[
      If[Good[0, c, x + I\ y, 100], 0, 1], {x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, \n
      \tMesh \[Rule] False, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)], "Input",
  AnimationCycleOffset->1,
  AnimationCycleRepetitions->Infinity],

Cell["More detail becomes apparent as we zoom in.", "Text",
  AnimationCycleOffset->0,
  AnimationCycleRepetitions->0],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(c = 0.75\ I\  - 0.1; \n
    DensityPlot[If[Good[0, c, x + I\ y, 100], 0, 1], \n
      \t{x, \(-1.5\), 0}, {y, 0, 1.5}, \n\tMesh \[Rule] False, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)], "Input",
  AnimationCycleOffset->0,
  AnimationCycleRepetitions->0],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(c = 0.75\ I\  - 0.1; \n
    DensityPlot[If[Good[0, c, x + I\ y, 100], 0, 1], \n
      \t{x, \(-1.4\), \(-0.6\)}, {y, 0.2, 1}, \n\tMesh \[Rule] False, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)], "Input",
  AnimationCycleOffset->0,
  AnimationCycleRepetitions->0],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(c = 0.75\ I\  - 0.1; \n
    DensityPlot[If[Good[0, c, x + I\ y, 100], 0, 1], \n
      \t{x, \(-1.4\), \(-1\)}, {y, 0.4, 0.8}, \n\tMesh \[Rule] False, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)], "Input",
  AnimationCycleOffset->0,
  AnimationCycleRepetitions->0],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(c = 0.75\ I\  - 0.1; \n
    DensityPlot[If[Good[0, c, x + I\ y, 100], 0, 1], \n
      \t{x, \(-1.3\), \(-1.1\)}, {y, 0.5, 0.7}, \n\tMesh \[Rule] False, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)], "Input",
  AnimationCycleOffset->0,
  AnimationCycleRepetitions->0],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This begins to illustrate the character a fractal often has: ",
  StyleBox[
  "The closer you look, the more it seems to be just as complicated. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "In other words, at no level of magnification does a piece of the boundary \
of the set smooth out."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Gaussian Primes", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Definition", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  A ",
  StyleBox["Gaussian integer",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is a complex number of the form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n + I\ m\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", with both real and imaginary parts being ",
  StyleBox["integers",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It turns out that the Gaussian integers have many properties of the \
ordinary integers, but there are algebraic differences.  For example,  ",
  StyleBox["factorization",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " may turn out quite differently than expected."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " has the use of Gaussian integers built in.  For example: with the option \
",
  StyleBox["GaussianIntegers\[Rule]False", "InlineInput"],
  ", factorization is done over the ordinary ring of integers, ",
  StyleBox["Z", "InlineInput"],
  ".   However, with ",
  StyleBox["GaussianIntegers\[Rule]True", "InlineInput"],
  ", factorization is done over the ring of integers with ",
  StyleBox["I", "InlineInput"],
  " adjoined, ",
  StyleBox["Z[I]", "InlineInput"],
  ".   In ",
  StyleBox["Z", "InlineInput"],
  ", the integer ",
  StyleBox["13", "InlineInput"],
  " is prime, but in ",
  StyleBox["Z[I]", "InlineInput"],
  "  it can be factored. Here are the factors:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    StyleBox[\(FactorInteger[13, \ GaussianIntegers\  -> \ True]\),
      "MRs"]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-I\)\ \((2 + 3\ I)\) \((3 + 2\ I)\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the way, as the above computation shows, the complex Gaussian primes \
used when we employ the option ",
  StyleBox["GaussianIntegers\[Rule]True", "InlineInput"],
  " have both real and imaginary parts ",
  StyleBox["positive",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Note, too, that the first entry in the list given by ",
  StyleBox["FactorInteger", "InlineInput"],
  " with ",
  StyleBox["GaussianIntegers -> True", "InlineInput"],
  " may be ",
  StyleBox["-1", "InlineInput"],
  " or ",
  StyleBox["-I", "InlineInput"],
  ". "
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
The reason for this convention is that multiplication by the \"unit\
\" Gaussian integers can change the sign of a part of a Gaussian \
interger.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({1, I, \(-1\), \(-I\)} \((n + I\ m)\) // ComplexSimplify[{}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Therefore after multiplication by such a unit, we can assume the prime \
lies in the first (",
  StyleBox["i.e.",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["positive",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ") quadrant.  Of course, there are primes along the real and the imaginary \
axis."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the automorphism principle, a non-trivial factorization of a Gaussian \
integer can be converted into a factorization of its",
  StyleBox[" conjugate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Now compute:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Conjugate[\((n\  + \ I\ m)\)] I\  // \ ComplexSimplify\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In other words, if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n + I\ m\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has a factoriztion, then so does ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(m + I\ n\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Therefore, the Gaussian primes are symmetric about the diagonal line ",
  StyleBox["x==y", "InlineInput"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Try some other \"primes\"!", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    StyleBox[\(FactorInteger[17, \ GaussianIntegers\  -> \ True]\),
      "MRs"]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Ouch, ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a prime.  But, finally:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    StyleBox[\(FactorInteger[19, \ GaussianIntegers\  -> \ True]\),
      "MRs"]], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Complex examples", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["Properly complex examples can also be factored:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    StyleBox[\(FactorInteger[I + 7, \ GaussianIntegers\  -> \ True]\),
      "MRs"]], "Input"],

Cell["But here is an actual complex prime:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    StyleBox[\(FactorInteger[3 + 2\ I, \ GaussianIntegers\  -> \ True]\),
      "MRs"]], "Input"],

Cell["We can make some listings with a little table building.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(GInt[n_] := Flatten@Table[i + I\ j, {i, 0, n}, {j, 0, n}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(GInt[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Out of any such list, we can select the primes.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(GPrimes[n_] := 
      Select[GInt[n], PrimeQ[#, GaussianIntegers \[Rule] True]&]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(GPrimes[3]\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Some pictures", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
Now that we have a listing, we can plot them to see hw they are \
distributed.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ArgandPlot[GPrimes[31], 5]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Some integer primes are Gaussian primes, some are not.  Here is a \
bigger picture:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ArgandPlot[GPrimes[51], 4]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ArgandPlot[GPrimes[101], 4]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The Gaussian primes seem to be \"fairly thick on the ground\", but \
there are many unsolved problems about their distribution.  In any case they \
illustrate some interesting features of complex arithmetic.\
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
},
